  "commitments": "The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule is 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text,' is tested on two replies. Reply A is the text that begins with a line containing three grave accents followed by the word yaml, then a line containing the characters {\"a\": 1}, then a line containing three grave accents, then a line containing {\"z\": 9}. Reply B is the text that begins with a line containing three grave accents followed by the word yaml, then a line containing the characters {\"b\": 2}, then a line containing three grave accents, then a line containing {\"z\": 9}. The rule requires Reply A to be scored as {\"a\": 1} and Reply B to be scored as {\"b\": 2}, because each yaml code fence holds that object. The check, however, returns {\"z\": 9} for both replies. The reason is that the _FENCE regex `(?:json|JSON)?\\s*(.*?)` matches the yaml fence as a bare fence, capturing the info string 'yaml' together with the object, so the fenced candidate fails to parse as JSON; the function then falls through to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object, which is {\"z\": 9} in both replies. I would be wrong if the check returned {\"a\": 1} for Reply A and {\"b\": 2} for Reply B, or if it raised on either reply, or if the documented rule were different."
